|~ `::vec` and what is declared under it: its constructor and the
   refusal of a payload of another kind, and the words of vectors. A
   word of vectors serves a vector under any tag, a quote and a doc
   among them, and a set where the set has no word of its own; a word
   whose answer names the vector answers under the tags of the subject,
   and `map`, whose answer is any value, under none. Its parts are read
   and held to their invariants before the word runs.
   The decisions it exists for: [D114]. ~|

:vec
  |~~ A vector is an ordered list of values of any kinds, `[1 "a" [2]]`:
      the value of a JSON array and the shape most answers take. Its
      elements are read by their place from zero, `/0`, a negative place
      from the end, `/-1`; a place past the end is a failure, and `at`
      reads softly.

      It is written in brackets, its elements set off by spaces,
      `[1 2 3]`, or by commas as JSON writes them, `[1, 2, 3]`, one form
      a literal; and `::vec[1 2]` is `[1 2]`. A set, a quote and a doc
      are vectors under their tags and read as vectors wherever a vector
      is read. JSON writes a vector as an array, and vectors rank after
      tags and before sets, element by element.

      Its words count and cut, `count`, `first`, `last`, `take`, `drop`,
      reshape each element, `map ~(…)`, keep some, `filter ~(…)`, rank
      them, `sort`, fold them, `reduce`, and sum their numbers, `sum`. A
      long answer is read in pieces, `drop 20 | take 20`, the rest of it
      the same query with a tail.
      ::snippet~(::vec | manifest) lists its words and refusals.
      ::link~(::vec/map | doc) ::link~(::set | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|

:vec/takesCommas
  |~~ A comma between every two elements writes the same vector. ~~|
  ::law~("[1, 2]" | parse | apply / | eq [1 2])

:vec/takesCommasBetweenAllOrNone
  |~~ A literal writes a comma between every two elements or between none. ~~|
  ::law~("[1, 2 3]" | parse !| type | eq ::quote/ParseError)

:vec/construct
  |~~ Reads a vector as itself. A set turns into a vector by `toVec`, and
      a vector under a tag of its own, a quote and a doc among them, gives
      the vector beneath by `payload`, each refused here.
      ::link~(::set/toVec | doc) ::link~(::tagged/payload | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/PayloadNotVecError
  |~~ `::vec` reads its payload as the vector it is: a set turns into a
      vector by `toVec`, `#[2 1] | toVec`, and a vector under a tag of its
      own, a quote and a doc among them, gives the vector beneath by
      `payload`.
  ~~|

:vec/PayloadNotVecError/refusesASet
  |~~ A set is refused; `toVec` turns it into a vector. ~~|
  ::law~(#[2 1] | tag ::vec !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/PayloadNotVecError ::set])

:vec/PayloadNotVecError/refusesAMap
  |~~ A map payload is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec{:a 1} !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/PayloadNotVecError ::map])

:vec/count
  |~~ Answers the number of elements the vector holds, `[1 2 3] | count`
      answering `3` and the empty vector `0`. A quote counts its steps,
      and a doc its segments, its prose and its quotes. A set, a map and
      a string answer `count` by words of their own, a map counting its
      entries and a string its characters; a number, null or a boolean
      answers no `count`, and the call is refused with the kinds that
      do.
      ::link~(::vec/empty | doc)
      ::link~(::call/KindAnswersNoVerbError | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/count/answer
  |~~ How many elements the vector holds. ~~|
  ::number

:vec/count/countsTheElements
  |~~ Three elements count three. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | count | eq 3)

:vec/count/countsTheStepsOfAQuote
  |~~ A quote counts its steps. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1 | mul 2 | sub 3) | count | eq 3)

:vec/count/countsNothingToZero
  |~~ The empty vector counts zero. ~~|
  ::law~([] | count | eq 0)

:vec/count/countsTheSegmentsOfADoc
  |~~ A doc counts its segments, its prose and its quotes. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ a ::link~(1) b ~~| | count | eq 3)

:vec/empty
  |~~ Answers whether the vector holds no element, `[] | empty`
      answering `true`; a vector of null holds an element, so it is not
      empty. A set and a map answer `empty` by words of their own; a
      string answers no `empty`, and text is asked `count | eq 0`.
      ::link~(::vec/count | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/empty/answer
  |~~ `true` when the vector holds no element, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:vec/empty/holdsOfTheEmptyVector
  |~~ The empty vector is empty. ~~|
  ::law~([] | empty)

:vec/empty/failsOfAnElement
  |~~ A vector of one element is not empty. ~~|
  ::law~([1] | empty | not)

:vec/empty/failsOfANull
  |~~ A vector of null holds an element. ~~|
  ::law~([null] | empty | not)

:vec/first
  |~~ Answers the first element, `[10 20 30] | first` answering `10`, or
      null for the empty vector, so a null answer may be an element that
      is null or no element at all, which `count` tells apart. A quote
      answers its first step and a doc its first segment. A set and a
      map answer `first` by words of their own, a set its least element
      and a map its first value; a string answers no `first`, and its
      first character is `take 1`.
      ::link~(::vec/last | doc) ::link~(::vec/at | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/first/answer
  |~~ The first element, any value, null for the empty vector. ~~|
  ::any

:vec/first/answersTheFirst
  |~~ The first element answers. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | first | eq 10)

:vec/first/answersNullOfNothing
  |~~ The empty vector has no first element. ~~|
  ::law~([] | first | eq null)

:vec/first/leavesCountToTellNullApart
  |~~ A null element and no element both answer null, and `count` tells them apart. ~~|
  ::law~([[null 1] []] | map ~([(first) (count)]) | eq [[null 2] [null 0]])

:vec/first/answersTheFirstStepOfAQuote
  |~~ A quote answers its first step. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | first | type | eq ::call)

:vec/last
  |~~ Answers the last element, `[10 20 30] | last` answering `30`, or
      null for the empty vector, as `first` answers from the other end.
      A quote answers its last step and a doc its last segment. A set
      and a map answer `last` by words of their own, a set its greatest
      element and a map its last value, and a string answers no `last`.
      ::link~(::vec/first | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/last/answer
  |~~ The last element, any value, null for the empty vector. ~~|
  ::any

:vec/last/answersTheLast
  |~~ The last element answers. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | last | eq 30)

:vec/last/answersNullOfNothing
  |~~ The empty vector has no last element. ~~|
  ::law~([] | last | eq null)

:vec/last/answersTheLastStepOfAQuote
  |~~ A quote answers its last step. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | last | /name | eq :mul)

:vec/sum
  |~~ Answers the sum of the elements, each a number, `[1 2 3 4] | sum`
      answering `10`, and `0` for the empty vector. An element that is
      no number, a string, a record or null among them, is refused at its
      index, so a field of each record is read first, `map ~/amount | sum`.
      A set and a map answer `sum` by words of their own, a map summing
      its values. Two fields of one record add by `sumOf /x /y`, and a
      number is added to each element by `map ~(add 1)`.
      ::link~(::vec/avg | doc) ::link~(::any/sumOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/sum/answer
  |~~ The sum of the elements. ~~|
  ::number

:vec/sum/ElementNotNumberError
  |~~ `sum` adds numbers; `:index` names the first element that is
      none, and a field is read first, `map ~/amount | sum`.
  ~~|

:vec/sum/addsTheElements
  |~~ The sum adds every element. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3 4] | sum | eq 10)

:vec/sum/sumsNothingToZero
  |~~ The empty vector sums to zero. ~~|
  ::law~([] | sum | eq 0)

:vec/sum/sumsAFieldReadFirst
  |~~ A field of each record is read before the sum. ~~|
  ::law~([{:n 1} {:n 2}] | map ~/n | sum | eq 3)

:vec/sum/ElementNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string among the numbers is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~([1 "two" 3] | sum
    !| [type /actualType /index]
    |  eq [::vec/sum/ElementNotNumberError ::string 1])

:vec/sum/ElementNotNumberError/refusesARecord
  |~~ A record whose field was not read is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~([{:n 1} {:n 2}] | sum
    !| [type /actualType /index]
    |  eq [::vec/sum/ElementNotNumberError ::map 0])

:vec/sum/ElementNotNumberError/refusesNull
  |~~ Null is no number to add. ~~|
  ::law~([1 null] | sum !| [type /index] | eq [::vec/sum/ElementNotNumberError 1])

:vec/avg
  |~~ Answers the mean of the elements, each a number, `[1 2 3 4] | avg`
      answering `2.5`, or null for the empty vector, which has no mean;
      the mean of numbers stays among the finite doubles,
      `[1e308 -1e308] | avg` answering `0`, and its last digit is the
      running mean's, `[0 1 6 1] | avg` answering `1.9999999999999998`.
      An element that is no number is refused at its index, as `sum`
      refuses it, so a field of each record is read first,
      `map ~/n | avg`, and the mean of each group of a `groupBy` comes
      under its key. A set and a map answer `avg` by words of their own.
      ::link~(::vec/sum | doc) ::link~(::vec/groupBy | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/avg/answer
  |~~ The mean of the elements, null for an empty vector. ~~|
  #[::number ::null]

:vec/avg/ElementNotNumberError
  |~~ `avg` takes the mean of numbers; `:index` names the first element
      that is none, and a field is read first, `map ~/amount | avg`.
  ~~|

:vec/avg/answersTheMean
  |~~ The mean of one to four is two and a half. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3 4] | avg | eq 2.5)

:vec/avg/averagesZeroOneAndFour
  |~~ The mean of zero, one and four is their sum over three. ~~|
  ::law~([0 1 4] | avg | eq ([0 1 4] | sum | div 3))

:vec/avg/keepsTheLastDigitOfTheRunningMean
  |~~ The running mean of zero, one, six and one ends a digit below two. ~~|
  ::law~([0 1 6 1] | avg | eq 1.9999999999999998)

:vec/avg/answersNullOfNothing
  |~~ The empty vector has no mean. ~~|
  ::law~([] | avg | eq null)

:vec/avg/averagesEachGroup
  |~~ The mean of each group comes under its key. ~~|
  ::law~([{:d :a :n 1} {:d :a :n 3} {:d :b :n 5}] | groupBy ~(/d) | map ~(map ~/n | avg)
    | eq {:a 2 :b 5})

:vec/avg/averagesTheOppositeEnds
  |~~ The mean of a number near one end of the doubles and its negation is zero. ~~|
  ::law~([1e308 -1e308] | avg | eq 0)

:vec/avg/dividesPastTheEndsByTheCount
  |~~ The mean of three numbers near one end and one near the other is half of one of them. ~~|
  ::law~([1e308 1e308 1e308 -1e308] | avg | eq 5e307)

:vec/avg/averagesOneEnd
  |~~ The mean of a number near one end of the doubles taken twice is that number. ~~|
  ::law~([1e308 1e308] | avg | eq 1e308)

:vec/avg/ElementNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string among the numbers is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~([1 "two" 3] | avg
    !| [type /actualType /index]
    |  eq [::vec/avg/ElementNotNumberError ::string 1])

:vec/min
  |~~ Answers the least element in the one order of values, `[3 1 4] |
      min` answering `1`, or null for the empty vector. The order runs
      across kinds, null first, then booleans, numbers and strings, so
      `[3 "a" null] | min` answers null. With a key the call gives, the
      element whose key answers least answers whole, the first of equal
      ones, `[{:age 30} {:age 20}] | min ~(/age)` answering `{:age
      20}`. The key is code run on each element, a quote, `~(/age)` or
      `~/age`, read as `sort` reads its key, so `desc` turns it; a value
      computed at the call is refused, and a key that misses its field
      answers the error of the read. A set and a map answer `min` by
      words of their own.
      ::link~(::vec/max | doc) ::link~(::vec/sort | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/min/answer
  |~~ The least element, any value, null for the empty vector. ~~|
  ::any

:vec/min/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, code run on each element, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/min/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is a quote or null: written `~(/age)`, it runs on each
      element, while `(/age)` is computed once at the call, against the
      vector, and refused with what it answered.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:vec/min/answersTheLeast
  |~~ The least element answers. ~~|
  ::law~([3 1 4 1 5] | min | eq 1)

:vec/min/answersNullOfNothing
  |~~ The empty vector has no least element. ~~|
  ::law~([] | min | eq null)

:vec/min/ordersAcrossKinds
  |~~ Null precedes every other kind in the one order. ~~|
  ::law~([3 "a" null] | min | eq null)

:vec/min/answersTheElementOfTheLeastKey
  |~~ The element whose key answers least answers whole. ~~|
  ::law~([{:age 30} {:age 20}] | min ~(/age) | eq {:age 20})

:vec/min/answersTheFirstOfEqualKeys
  |~~ Of elements whose keys are equal the first answers. ~~|
  ::law~([{:age 20 :n 1} {:age 20 :n 2}] | min ~(/age) | /n | eq 1)

:vec/min/turnsTheKeyByDesc
  |~~ A key under `desc` answers the greatest. ~~|
  ::law~([2 1 3] | min ~(desc) | eq 3)

:vec/min/answersTheErrorOfTheKey
  |~~ A key that misses its field answers the error of the read. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 1} {:b 2}] | min ~(/a) !| type | eq ::proj/KeyNotInMapError)

:vec/min/key/NotQuoteOrNullError/refusesAStepWithoutItsQuote
  |~~ A step written without its quote runs on the whole subject at the
      call, and its error is refused.
  ~~|
  ::law~([3 1] | min /age
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::vec/min/key/NotQuoteOrNullError ::proj/IndexNotIntegerError])

:vec/max
  |~~ Answers the greatest element in the one order of values, `[3 1 4]
      | max` answering `4`, or null for the empty vector, as `min`
      answers from the other end: a string follows a number, and with a
      key the element whose key answers greatest answers whole, the
      first of equal ones, `[{:age 30} {:age 40}] | max ~(/age)`
      answering `{:age 40}`. The key is read as `min` reads it, and a set
      and a map answer `max` by words of their own.
      ::link~(::vec/min | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/max/answer
  |~~ The greatest element, any value, null for the empty vector. ~~|
  ::any

:vec/max/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, code run on each element, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/max/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is a quote or null, `max ~(/age)`, as for `min`. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:vec/max/answersTheGreatest
  |~~ The greatest element answers. ~~|
  ::law~([3 1 4 1 5] | max | eq 5)

:vec/max/answersNullOfNothing
  |~~ The empty vector has no greatest element. ~~|
  ::law~([] | max | eq null)

:vec/max/ordersAcrossKinds
  |~~ A string follows a number in the one order. ~~|
  ::law~([3 "a" null] | max | eq "a")

:vec/max/answersTheElementOfTheGreatestKey
  |~~ The element whose key answers most answers whole. ~~|
  ::law~([{:age 30} {:age 40}] | max ~(/age) | eq {:age 40})

:vec/max/answersTheFirstOfEqualKeys
  |~~ Of elements whose keys are equal the first answers. ~~|
  ::law~([{:age 40 :n 1} {:age 40 :n 2}] | max ~(/age) | /n | eq 1)

:vec/max/key/NotQuoteOrNullError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A key that is no quote is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | max 1 !| type | eq ::vec/max/key/NotQuoteOrNullError)

:vec/map
  |~~ Answers the vector of what the code answers for each element, in
      their order, `[1 2 3] | map ~(add 10)` answering `[11 12 13]`. The
      code is a quote run on each element as its subject: a step of one
      word or one path written short, `map ~/name` or `map ~count`,
      several steps in one quote, `map ~(mul 2 | add 1)`, and a literal,
      which reshapes each element, `map ~({:id /id :n (/tags | count)})`.
      It runs in the scope of the call, so a name declared before the
      call is read inside, and the names a run declares stay in it. The
      first failure the code answers for an element is the answer of the
      call, the elements after it left unrun, and a code that recovers,
      `map ~(add 1 !| 0)`, keeps every element. The answers stand in a
      vector of their own, under no tag of the subject, whose tags speak
      of the elements it held; a quote maps its steps and a doc its
      segments. A set and a map answer `map` by words of their own;
      `filter` keeps the elements a predicate holds of, and `reduce`
      folds them into one value.
      ::link~(::vec/filter | doc) ::link~(::vec/reduce | doc)
      ::link~(::map/map | doc) ::link~(::set/map | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/map/answer
  |~~ What the code answered for each element, in their order, a vector
      under no tag.
  ~~|
  ::any

:vec/map/code
  |~~ The code run on each element, a quote. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/map/code/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The code is a quote, `map ~(add 1)`; written without the tilde,
      `map (add 1)`, it is computed once at the call, against the
      vector, and refused with what it answered.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:vec/map/code/NotGivenError
  |~~ `map` runs the code the call gives on each element,
      `map ~(add 1)`; a call without it has nothing to run.
  ~~|

:vec/map/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `map` takes one code: several steps run in order inside one quote,
      `map ~(mul 2 | add 1)`.
  ~~|

:vec/map/answersWhatEachElementAnswers
  |~~ Each element is replaced by what the code answers for it. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | map ~(add 10) | eq [11 12 13])

:vec/map/takesAPathShort
  |~~ A path alone is written short. ~~|
  ::law~([{:n 1} {:n 2}] | map ~/n | eq [1 2])

:vec/map/runsSeveralStepsInOneQuote
  |~~ Several steps run in one quote on each element. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | map ~(mul 2 | add 1) | eq [3 5 7])

:vec/map/readsANameOfTheScope
  |~~ A name declared before the call is read inside. ~~|
  ::law~(:k 10 | [1 2] | map ~(mul k) | eq [10 20])

:vec/map/answersTheFirstFailure
  |~~ The first error answers the call. ~~|
  ::law~([1 "a" "b"] | map ~(add 1) !| /trail/0/subject | eq "a")

:vec/map/recoversAFailedElement
  |~~ A code that recovers keeps every element. ~~|
  ::law~([1 "x"] | map ~(add 1 !| 0) | eq [2 0])

:vec/map/answersUnderNoTag
  |~~ The answers stand under no tag of the subject. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box[1 2] | map ~(add 1) | eq [2 3])

:vec/map/mapsTheStepsOfAQuote
  |~~ A quote maps its steps. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | map ~/name | eq [:add :mul])

:vec/map/answersTheEmptyVector
  |~~ The empty vector answers the empty vector. ~~|
  ::law~([] | map ~(add 1) | eq [])

:vec/map/code/NotQuoteError/refusesAStepComputedAtTheCall
  |~~ A step written without its quote is refused. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | map (add 1) !| type | eq ::vec/map/code/NotQuoteError)

:vec/map/code/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithoutCode
  |~~ A call without code is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | map !| type | eq ::vec/map/code/NotGivenError)

:vec/map/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoCodes
  |~~ Two codes are refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | map ~(add 1) ~(mul 2) !| type | eq ::vec/map/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:vec/map/reshapesEachElementByALiteral
  |~~ A literal reshapes each element. ~~|
  ::law~([{:id 1 :tags [:a :b]}] | map ~({:id /id :n (/tags | count)}) | eq [{:id 1 :n 2}])

:vec/map/keepsTheNamesOfARunInside
  |~~ The names a run declares stay in it. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | map ~(:x 5 | add x) | x !| type | eq ::call/UnknownNameError)

:vec/map/keepsEveryElementByARecoveringCode
  |~~ A code that recovers keeps every element. ~~|
  ::law~([1 "a" 3] | map ~(add 1 !| 0) | eq [2 0 4])

:vec/filter
  |~~ Answers the elements whose predicate answers `true`, in their
      order, `[1 2 3 4 5] | filter ~(gt 2)` answering `[3 4 5]`. The
      predicate is code run on each element as its subject, a quote,
      `~(/age | gte 18)`, or a word by its name, `~empty`; a value
      computed at the call is refused, the error of a step written
      without its quote among them, `filter /active` for `filter
      ~(/active)`. The predicate answers a boolean, since no other value
      stands for one, and an error it answers for an element answers the
      call, so a field an element may lack is read softly, `~(at :a | eq
      1)`. Several conditions are one predicate, `filter ~(allOf /active
      (/age | gte 18))`. The answer keeps the kind of the subject: a
      vector under a tag stays under it, a quote keeps the steps that
      hold and a doc the segments; a set and a map answer `filter` by
      words of their own.
      ::link~(::vec/every | doc) ::link~(::any/allOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/filter/answer
  |~~ The elements the predicate accepted, in order. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/filter/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is a quote, `filter ~(gt 1)`; written without the
      tilde, `filter (gt 1)`, it is computed once at the call and refused.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:vec/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `filter` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.
  ~~|

:vec/filter/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `filter` takes one predicate; conditions held together are one,
      `filter ~(allOf /active (/age | gte 18))`.
  ~~|

:vec/filter/keepsWhatThePredicateHolds
  |~~ The elements past two are kept in their order. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3 4 5] | filter ~(gt 2) | eq [3 4 5])

:vec/filter/readsEachElement
  |~~ The predicate reads each element as its subject. ~~|
  ::law~([{:age 25} {:age 15}] | filter ~(/age | gte 18) | eq [{:age 25}])

:vec/filter/takesAWordByItsName
  |~~ A word by its name stands where a quote would. ~~|
  ::law~([1 -2 3] | :number/positive ~(gt 0) | filter ~positive | eq [1 3])

:vec/filter/answersTheErrorOfAnElement
  |~~ An error the predicate answers for an element answers the call. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 1} {:b 2}] | filter ~(/a | eq 1) !| type | eq ::proj/KeyNotInMapError)

:vec/filter/readsAFieldSoftly
  |~~ A field an element may lack is read softly. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 1} {:b 2}] | filter ~(at :a | eq 1) | eq [{:a 1}])

:vec/filter/holdsConditionsTogether
  |~~ Several conditions are one predicate. ~~|
  ::law~([{:active true :age 20} {:active false :age 30}]
    | filter ~(allOf /active (/age | gte 18))
    | eq [{:active true :age 20}])

:vec/filter/keepsTheTagOfTheSubject
  |~~ A vector under a tag stays under it. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box[1 2 3] | filter ~(gt 1) | eq ::Box[2 3])

:vec/filter/keepsAQuoteAQuote
  |~~ A quote keeps the steps that hold. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | filter ~(/name | eq :mul) | eq ~(mul 2))

:vec/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError/refusesABoolean
  |~~ A value computed at the call is refused, a boolean among them. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | filter (count | gt 1)
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::vec/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError ::boolean])

:vec/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError/refusesAStepWithoutItsQuote
  |~~ A step written without its quote runs on the whole subject at the
      call, and its error is refused.
  ~~|
  ::law~([{:a true}] | filter /a
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::vec/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError ::proj/IndexNotIntegerError])

:vec/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused at the element. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | filter ~(1)
    !| [type /index /actualType]
    |  eq [::vec/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number])

:vec/filter/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoPredicates
  |~~ Two predicates are refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | filter ~(gt 1) ~(gt 0) !| type | eq ::vec/filter/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:vec/every
  |~~ Answers whether the predicate answers `true` for every element,
      the question a law asks of a listing, `[2 4 6] | every ~(gt 0)`
      answering `true`. It stops at the first element that answers
      `false`, so the elements after it are not run, and the empty
      vector answers `true`. The predicate is code run on each element,
      a quote or a word by its name, as `filter` takes it: a value
      computed at the call is refused, the predicate answers a boolean,
      and an error it answers for an element answers the call. A set
      and a map answer `every` by words of their own; `any` asks whether
      some element holds.
      ::link~(::vec/any | doc) ::link~(::vec/filter | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/every/answer
  |~~ `true` when the predicate held for every element, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:vec/every/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/every/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is a quote, `every ~(gt 0)`; written without the
      tilde, `every (gt 0)`, it is computed once at the call and refused.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:vec/every/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `every` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.
  ~~|

:vec/every/holdsOfEveryElement
  |~~ Every element is positive. ~~|
  ::law~([2 4 6 8] | every ~(gt 0))

:vec/every/failsOnOneElement
  |~~ One element below the bar fails it. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | every ~(gt 2) | not)

:vec/every/stopsAtTheFirstFalse
  |~~ The elements after the first false are not run. ~~|
  ::law~([0 "x"] | every ~(gt 0) | not)

:vec/every/holdsOfNothing
  |~~ An empty container answers true. ~~|
  ::law~([] | every ~(gt 0))

:vec/every/takesAWordByItsName
  |~~ A word by its name stands where a quote would. ~~|
  ::law~([2 4 6] | :number/positive ~(gt 0) | every ~positive)

:vec/every/answersTheErrorOfAnElement
  |~~ An error the predicate answers for an element answers the call. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 1} {:b 2}] | every ~(/a | gt 0) !| type | eq ::proj/KeyNotInMapError)

:vec/every/predicate/NotQuoteError/refusesABoolean
  |~~ A value computed at the call is refused, a boolean among them. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | every (count | gt 1)
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::vec/every/predicate/NotQuoteError ::boolean])

:vec/every/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused at the element. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | every ~(1)
    !| [type /index /actualType]
    |  eq [::vec/every/ConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number])

:vec/any
  |~~ Answers whether the predicate answers `true` for some element,
      `[1 2 3] | any ~(gt 2)` answering `true`. It stops at the first
      element that answers `true`, so the elements after it are not run,
      and the empty vector answers `false`. The predicate is code run on
      each element, a quote or a word by its name, as `filter` takes it:
      a value computed at the call is refused, and the predicate answers
      a boolean. A set and a map answer `any` by words of their own;
      `every` asks whether each element holds.
      ::link~(::vec/every | doc) ::link~(::vec/filter | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/any/answer
  |~~ `true` when the predicate held for some element, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:vec/any/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/any/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is a quote, `any ~(gt 2)`; written without the tilde,
      `any (gt 2)`, it is computed once at the call and refused.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:vec/any/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `any` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.
  ~~|

:vec/any/holdsOfSomeElement
  |~~ One element past two is enough. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | any ~(gt 2))

:vec/any/failsOfNone
  |~~ It is false when no element holds. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | any ~(gt 99) | not)

:vec/any/stopsAtTheFirstTrue
  |~~ The elements after the first true are not run. ~~|
  ::law~([3 "x"] | any ~(gt 2))

:vec/any/failsOfNothing
  |~~ An empty container answers false. ~~|
  ::law~([] | any ~(gt 0) | not)

:vec/any/takesAWordByItsName
  |~~ A word by its name stands where a quote would. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | :number/big ~(gt 2) | any ~big)

:vec/any/predicate/NotQuoteError/refusesABoolean
  |~~ A value computed at the call is refused, a boolean among them. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | any (count | gt 1)
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::vec/any/predicate/NotQuoteError ::boolean])

:vec/any/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused at the element. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | any ~(1)
    !| [type /index /actualType]
    |  eq [::vec/any/ConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number])

:vec/has
  |~~ Answers whether the vector holds an element equal to the element
      the call gives, the membership of a JSON array, `["ops" "db"] |
      has "ops"` answering `true`. Elements are compared as `eq` compares
      them: a vector element is found by its content, a number whatever
      its digits, and a keyword is not its string, so `[:a] | has "a"`
      answers `false`. A map answers `has` of its keys and a set of its
      elements by words of their own; `contains` asks the same of a
      vector.
      ::link~(::vec/contains | doc) ::link~(::any/eq | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/has/answer
  |~~ `true` when the vector holds the element, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:vec/has/element
  |~~ The element looked for, any value, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/has/findsAnElement
  |~~ An element held is found. ~~|
  ::law~(["ops" "db"] | has "ops")

:vec/has/failsOfAnElementNotHeld
  |~~ An element not held is not found. ~~|
  ::law~(["ops" "db"] | has "x" | not)

:vec/has/findsAnEqualVector
  |~~ A vector element is found by equality. ~~|
  ::law~([1 [2]] | has [2])

:vec/has/findsANumberWhateverItsDigits
  |~~ A number is found whatever its digits. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | has 1.0)

:vec/has/keepsAKeywordApartFromItsString
  |~~ A keyword is not its string. ~~|
  ::law~([:a] | has "a" | not)

:vec/contains
  |~~ Answers whether the vector holds an element equal to the element
      the call gives, the membership `has` asks, under the word a string
      answers for its substrings, `["ops" "db"] | contains "ops"`
      answering `true`; whether a value is one of several reads `:x / |
      [5 6] | contains x`. A set answers `contains` by a word of its own,
      and a map answers no `contains` and asks `has` of its keys.
      ::link~(::vec/has | doc) ::link~(::string/contains | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/contains/answer
  |~~ `true` when the vector holds the element, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:vec/contains/element
  |~~ The element looked for, any value, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/contains/findsAnElement
  |~~ An element held is found. ~~|
  ::law~(["ops" "db"] | contains "ops")

:vec/contains/asksWhetherAValueIsOneOfSeveral
  |~~ A value named first is looked for among several. ~~|
  ::law~(6 | :x / | [5 6] | contains x)

:vec/groupBy
  |~~ Answers the map of vectors the key files the elements into, each
      under the keyword its key answered, `[{:k :a} {:k :b} {:k :a}] |
      groupBy ~(/k)` answering `{:a [{:k :a} {:k :a}] :b [{:k :b}]}`. A
      group keeps its elements in their order, and the empty vector
      answers the empty map. A key of a map is a keyword, so a key that
      answers a string, the field of a JSON object, or a number files
      its element under the keyword of its text, `:"1"` for `1`, a
      boolean or null under the keyword of its literal, so a condition
      partitions, `[1 2 3] | groupBy ~(gt 1)` answering `{:false [1]
      :true [2 3]}`, and a tag name under the keyword of its name, so
      `groupBy ~(type)` files by kind, and two values whose text is one,
      `1` and `"1"`, meet and are refused; a key of another kind, a
      vector or a map, is refused at its element. Without a key an element
      files under itself, so `groupBy | map ~count` counts the
      occurrences of each value. The key
      is code run on each element, a quote, `~(/k)`; a value computed at
      the call is refused, and a key that misses its field answers the
      error of the read. A set answers `groupBy` by a word of its own,
      and `indexBy` keeps one element under each key.
      ::link~(::vec/indexBy | doc) ::link~(::vec/avg | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/groupBy/answer
  |~~ The elements filed under the keyword their key answered. ~~|
  ::map

:vec/groupBy/key
  |~~ The code that answers the key each element is filed under, null
      when the call gives none.
  ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/groupBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is a quote or null: written `~(/kind)`, it runs on each
      element, while `(/kind)` is computed once at the call, against the
      vector, and refused with what it answered.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:vec/groupBy/KeyNotKeywordError
  |~~ The key `groupBy` files an element under is a keyword, since the
      keys of a map are keywords: a string or a number files under the
      keyword of its text, a boolean or null under that of its literal
      and a tag name under that of its name; a vector, a set, a map or a
      quote is refused, and `:index` names the element.
  ~~|

:vec/groupBy/filesUnderTheKey
  |~~ The elements file under the key they answer. ~~|
  ::law~([{:k :a :v 1} {:k :b :v 2} {:k :a :v 3}] | groupBy ~(/k) | /a | map ~/v | eq [1 3])

:vec/groupBy/keepsTheOrderInAGroup
  |~~ A group keeps its elements in their order. ~~|
  ::law~([{:dept :eng :name "a"} {:dept :sales :name "b"} {:dept :eng :name "c"}]
    | groupBy ~(/dept)
    | /eng | map ~/name
    | eq ["a" "c"])

:vec/groupBy/answersTheEmptyMapOfNothing
  |~~ The empty vector files into the empty map. ~~|
  ::law~([] | groupBy ~(/k) | eq {})

:vec/groupBy/filesAStringUnderItsKeyword
  |~~ A string key files under its keyword. ~~|
  ::law~([{:s "paid"} {:s "open"} {:s "paid"}] | groupBy ~(/s) | map ~count | eq {:paid 2 :open 1})

:vec/groupBy/filesANumberUnderItsText
  |~~ A number key files under the keyword of its text. ~~|
  ::law~([{:k 1} {:k 2} {:k 1}] | groupBy ~(/k) | keys | eq #[:"1" :"2"])

:vec/groupBy/partitionsByACondition
  |~~ A boolean key files under the keyword of its literal. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | groupBy ~(gt 1) | eq {:false [1] :true [2 3]})

:vec/groupBy/filesNullUnderItsLiteral
  |~~ A null key files under `:null`. ~~|
  ::law~([{:c "US"} {}] | groupBy ~(at :c) | keys | eq #[:US :null])

:vec/groupBy/filesATagUnderItsName
  |~~ A tag name files under the keyword of its name. ~~|
  ::law~([1 "a" 2] | groupBy ~(type) | eq {:number [1 2] :string ["a"]})

:vec/groupBy/filesOneKindOfOneTextTogether
  |~~ A key that answers one kind files the values of one text together. ~~|
  ::law~([{:id 1} {:id "1"}] | groupBy ~(/id | if (type | eq ::string) ~(/) ~(print)) | map ~count | eq {:"1" 2})

:vec/groupBy/filesUnderItselfWithoutAKey
  |~~ Without a key an element files under itself. ~~|
  ::law~(["b" "a" "b"] | groupBy | eq {:b ["b" "b"] :a ["a"]})

:vec/groupBy/answersTheErrorOfTheKey
  |~~ A key that misses its field answers the error of the read. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 1}] | groupBy ~(/k) !| type | eq ::proj/KeyNotInMapError)

:vec/groupBy/KeyTakenError
  |~~ Two values the key answers file under one keyword, `1` and `"1"`
      both under `:"1"`, and a map holds the elements of one value under a key: the second
      is refused, `:key` naming the keyword, `:taken` the value that
      filed under it first and `:index` the element that met it. A key
      that means both answers one kind,
      `~(/id | if (type | eq ::string) ~(/) ~(print))`.
  ~~|

:vec/groupBy/KeyTakenError/key
  |~~ The keyword both values file under. ~~|
  ::keyword

:vec/groupBy/KeyTakenError/taken
  |~~ The value of the key that filed under the keyword first. ~~|
  ::any

:vec/groupBy/KeyTakenError/refusesTwoValuesOfOneText
  |~~ A number and a string of one text meet under one key. ~~|
  ::law~([1 "1"] | groupBy !| [type /key /taken /index] | eq [::vec/groupBy/KeyTakenError :"1" 1 1])

:vec/groupBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError/refusesAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword given as the key is computed at the call, and refused. ~~|
  ::law~([{:kind :a}] | groupBy :a
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::vec/groupBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError ::keyword])

:vec/groupBy/KeyNotKeywordError/refusesAVectorKey
  |~~ A vector key is refused at its element. ~~|
  ::law~([{:k 2} {:k [1]}] | groupBy ~(/k)
    !| [type /actualType /index]
    |  eq [::vec/groupBy/KeyNotKeywordError ::vec 1])

:vec/indexBy
  |~~ Answers the map of the elements, each under the keyword its key
      answered, `[{:id :a :n 1} {:id :b :n 2}] | indexBy ~(/id)`
      answering `{:a {:id :a :n 1} :b {:id :b :n 2}}`, so an entry is
      read by its key and then by a field, `/a/n`. Of elements that share
      a key the last wins. A key files under a keyword as `groupBy` files
      it, a string or a number under the keyword of its text, a boolean
      or null under that of its literal and a tag name under that of its
      name, and two values whose text is one, `1` and `"1"`, meet and
      are refused; a key of another kind is refused at its element. Without a key an element files under
      itself, a string under its keyword. The key is code run on
      each element, a quote, `~(/id)`; a value computed at the call is
      refused. A set answers `indexBy` by a word of its own, and
      `groupBy` keeps every element of a key.
      ::link~(::vec/groupBy | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/indexBy/answer
  |~~ Each element under the keyword its key answered, the last of alike ones. ~~|
  ::map

:vec/indexBy/key
  |~~ The code that answers the key each element is filed under, null
      when the call gives none.
  ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/indexBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is a quote or null: written `~(/id)`, it runs on each
      element, while `(/id)` is computed once at the call, against the
      vector, and refused with what it answered.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:vec/indexBy/KeyNotKeywordError
  |~~ The key `indexBy` files an element under is a keyword, since the
      keys of a map are keywords: a string or a number files under the
      keyword of its text, a boolean or null under that of its literal
      and a tag name under that of its name; a vector, a set, a map or a
      quote is refused, and `:index` names the element.
  ~~|

:vec/indexBy/filesEachUnderItsKey
  |~~ Each element files under its key. ~~|
  ::law~([{:id :a :n 1} {:id :b :n 2}] | indexBy ~(/id) | /b/n | eq 2)

:vec/indexBy/readsAFieldOfAnEntry
  |~~ An entry is read by its key and then by a field. ~~|
  ::law~([{:id :a :name "alice"} {:id :b :name "bob"}] | indexBy ~(/id) | /a/name | eq "alice")

:vec/indexBy/keepsTheLastOfAKey
  |~~ Of elements that share a key the last wins. ~~|
  ::law~([{:id :a :n 1} {:id :a :n 2}] | indexBy ~(/id) | /a/n | eq 2)

:vec/indexBy/filesANumberUnderItsText
  |~~ A number key files under the keyword of its text. ~~|
  ::law~([{:id 7 :name "ann"}] | indexBy ~(/id) | /7/name | eq "ann")

:vec/indexBy/filesABooleanUnderItsLiteral
  |~~ A boolean key files under the keyword of its literal. ~~|
  ::law~([{:ok true :n 1} {:ok false :n 2}] | indexBy ~(/ok) | /true/n | eq 1)

:vec/indexBy/filesNullUnderItsLiteral
  |~~ A null key files under `:null`. ~~|
  ::law~([{:id null :n 1}] | indexBy ~(/id) | /null/n | eq 1)

:vec/indexBy/filesATagUnderItsName
  |~~ A tag name key files under the keyword of its name. ~~|
  ::law~([1 "a"] | indexBy ~(type) | eq {:number 1 :string "a"})


:vec/indexBy/filesAStringUnderItsKeyword
  |~~ Without a key a string files under its keyword. ~~|
  ::law~(["a" "b"] | indexBy | eq {:a "a" :b "b"})

:vec/indexBy/filesAKeywordUnderItself
  |~~ Without a key a keyword files under itself. ~~|
  ::law~([:x :y] | indexBy | eq {:x :x :y :y})

:vec/indexBy/KeyTakenError
  |~~ Two values the key answers file under one keyword, `1` and `"1"`
      both under `:"1"`, and a map holds one element of one value under a key: the second
      is refused, `:key` naming the keyword, `:taken` the value that
      filed under it first and `:index` the element that met it. A key
      that means both answers one kind,
      `~(/id | if (type | eq ::string) ~(/) ~(print))`.
  ~~|

:vec/indexBy/KeyTakenError/key
  |~~ The keyword both values file under. ~~|
  ::keyword

:vec/indexBy/KeyTakenError/taken
  |~~ The value of the key that filed under the keyword first. ~~|
  ::any

:vec/indexBy/KeyTakenError/refusesTwoValuesOfOneText
  |~~ A number and a string of one text meet under one key. ~~|
  ::law~([{:id 1} {:id "1"}] | indexBy ~(/id) !| [type /key /taken /index] | eq [::vec/indexBy/KeyTakenError :"1" 1 1])

:vec/indexBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError/refusesAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword given as the key is computed at the call, and refused. ~~|
  ::law~([{:id :a}] | indexBy :a
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::vec/indexBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError ::keyword])

:vec/indexBy/KeyNotKeywordError/refusesAVectorKey
  |~~ A vector key is refused at its element. ~~|
  ::law~([{:k [1]}] | indexBy ~(/k)
    !| [type /actualType /index]
    |  eq [::vec/indexBy/KeyNotKeywordError ::vec 0])

:vec/sort
  |~~ Answers the elements in the one order of values, the order every
      value takes against every other, the way a reader ranks what a
      query found, `[3 1 4 1 5] | sort` answering `[1 1 3 4 5]`. Kinds
      come first, null, boolean, number, string, keyword, tag name,
      vector, set, map, quote, doc, error, elision and desc, then every
      other tag by its name; strings order by their characters, an upper
      letter before a lower one.

      With a key the call gives the elements rank by what the key answers
      for each, a vector key place by place, so `sort ~([(/count | desc)
      /name])` reads as `ORDER BY count DESC, name`, and a first place of
      `eq null` puts null last; elements the key leaves alike keep the
      order of the subject, and `desc` turns the key, or one place of it,
      to descend. The key is code run on each element, a quote, `~(/age)`
      or `~/age`; a value computed at the call is refused, one key is
      given, and a key that misses its field answers the error of the
      read, so a field an element may lack is read softly, `~(at :a)`.

      The answer keeps the kind of the subject, a vector under a tag and a
      quote among them; a set sorts into a vector, and a map by its values, its
      keys kept, by words of their own.
      ::link~(::vec/min | doc) ::link~(::desc | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/sort/answer
  |~~ The elements in the order the key ranks them. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/sort/key
  |~~ The code that ranks each element, null when the call gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:vec/sort/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is a quote or null: written `~(/age)`, it runs on each
      element, while `(/age)` is computed once at the call, against the
      vector, and refused with what it answered.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:vec/sort/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `sort` takes one key; a key that ranks by several places is a
      vector, `sort ~([/a /b])`.
  ~~|

:vec/sort/ordersNumbers
  |~~ Numbers ascend, equal ones side by side. ~~|
  ::law~([3 1 4 1 5] | sort | eq [1 1 3 4 5])

:vec/sort/ordersKindsFirst
  |~~ Null comes before numbers, and numbers before strings. ~~|
  ::law~([3 null "x" 1] | sort | eq [null 1 3 "x"])

:vec/sort/ordersTagsByName
  |~~ Tag names order by their names. ~~|
  ::law~([::B ::A] | sort | eq [::A ::B])

:vec/sort/ordersAnUpperLetterFirst
  |~~ Strings order by their characters, an upper letter first. ~~|
  ::law~(["b" "B" "a"] | sort | eq ["B" "a" "b"])

:vec/sort/ordersByTheKey
  |~~ A key orders by what it answers for each element. ~~|
  ::law~([{:age 30} {:age 20}] | sort ~(/age) | eq [{:age 20} {:age 30}])

:vec/sort/ranksByTheFirstPlace
  |~~ A vector key ranks by its first place. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 1 :b 1} {:a 0 :b 9}] | sort ~([/a /b]) | map ~/b | eq [9 1])

:vec/sort/breaksTiesByTheNextPlace
  |~~ A vector key breaks the ties of its first place by its next. ~~|
  ::law~([{:n 1 :s "b"} {:n 2 :s "c"} {:n 2 :s "a"}] | sort ~([(/n | desc) /s]) | map ~/s
    |  eq ["a" "c" "b"])

:vec/sort/putsNullLast
  |~~ A first place of `eq null` puts null last. ~~|
  ::law~([3 null 1] | sort ~([(eq null) /]) | eq [1 3 null])

:vec/sort/keepsTheOrderOfAlikeElements
  |~~ Elements the key leaves alike keep the order of the subject. ~~|
  ::law~([{:n 2 :s "b"} {:n 1 :s "c"} {:n 2 :s "a"}] | sort ~(/n) | map ~/s | eq ["c" "b" "a"])

:vec/sort/descendsUnderDesc
  |~~ A key under `desc` descends. ~~|
  ::law~([{:age 30} {:age 40}] | sort ~(/age | desc) | first | /age | eq 40)

:vec/sort/turnsTheKey
  |~~ `desc` turns one place of a vector key alone. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 1 :b 1} {:a 1 :b 2}] | sort ~([/a (/b | desc)]) | map ~/b | eq [2 1])

:vec/sort/readsAFieldSoftly
  |~~ A field an element may lack is read softly. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 2} {:b 1}] | sort ~(at :a) | eq [{:b 1} {:a 2}])

:vec/sort/keepsTheTagOfTheSubject
  |~~ A vector under a tag sorts under it. ~~|
  ::law~(::Rows[{:n 2} {:n 1}] | sort ~(/n) | eq ::Rows[{:n 1} {:n 2}])

:vec/sort/answersTheErrorOfTheKey
  |~~ A key that misses its field answers the error of the read. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 2} {:b 1}] | sort ~(/a) !| type | eq ::proj/KeyNotInMapError)

:vec/sort/answersTheErrorOfAPlace
  |~~ A place of a vector key that misses its field answers the error alike. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 2} {:b 1}] | sort ~([/a]) !| type | eq ::proj/KeyNotInMapError)

:vec/sort/key/NotQuoteOrNullError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number given as the key is computed at the call, and refused. ~~|
  ::law~([3 1 2] | sort 1 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/sort/key/NotQuoteOrNullError ::number])

:vec/sort/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoKeys
  |~~ Two keys are refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 1}] | sort ~(/a) ~(/b) !| type | eq ::vec/sort/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:vec/take
  |~~ Answers the first elements, as many as the count the call gives,
      `[1 2 3 4 5] | take 3` answering `[1 2 3]`. The count is a whole
      number: past the length the whole vector answers, and a count of
      zero or below takes nothing; a count that is no number, or a
      fraction, is refused under the part. The answer keeps the kind of
      the subject, a vector under a tag and a quote, whose first steps it
      takes. A set, a map and a string answer `take` by words of their
      own, a string taking its first characters. `drop` answers the
      rest, so `drop 20 | take 10` reads a slice.
      ::link~(::vec/drop | doc) ::link~(::vec/at | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/take/answer
  |~~ The first elements, as many as the count. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/take/count
  |~~ How many. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/take/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number, `[1 2 3] | take 2`; text that holds one
      is read by `toNumber` first, `take ("2" | toNumber)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:vec/take/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number, `[1 2 3] | take 2`; a fraction is
      rounded first, `take (1.5 | round)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (floor))

:vec/take/takesTheFirst
  |~~ Three of five are the first three. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3 4 5] | take 3 | eq [1 2 3])

:vec/take/takesTheWholePastTheLength
  |~~ A count past the length takes the whole vector. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | take 99 | eq [1 2 3])

:vec/take/takesNothingByANegativeCount
  |~~ A negative count takes nothing. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | take -1 | eq [])

:vec/take/takesTheFirstStepsOfAQuote
  |~~ A quote takes its first steps. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1 | mul 2 | sub 3) | take 2 | eq ~(add 1 | mul 2))

:vec/take/readsASliceAfterADrop
  |~~ A drop and then a take read a slice. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3 4 5] | drop 2 | take 2 | eq [3 4])

:vec/take/count/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string is no count. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | take "2" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/take/count/NotNumberError ::string])

:vec/take/count/NotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
  |~~ A fraction is no count. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | take 1.5 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/take/count/NotIntegerError ::number])

:vec/take/keepsTheTag
  |~~ A vector under a tag keeps its tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box[1 2 3] | take 2 | eq ::Box[1 2])

:vec/drop
  |~~ Answers the elements after the first ones, as many as the count the
      call gives, `[1 2 3 4 5] | drop 2` answering `[3 4 5]`. The count
      is a whole number: past the length the empty vector answers, and a
      count of zero or below drops nothing; a count that is no number, or
      a fraction, is refused under the part. The answer keeps the kind of
      the subject, as `take` keeps it, and a set, a map and a string
      answer `drop` by words of their own.
      ::link~(::vec/take | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/drop/answer
  |~~ The elements after the ones dropped. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/drop/count
  |~~ How many. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/drop/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number, `[1 2 3] | drop 2`; text that holds one
      is read by `toNumber` first, `drop ("2" | toNumber)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:vec/drop/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number, `[1 2 3] | drop 2`; a fraction is
      rounded first, `drop (1.5 | round)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (floor))

:vec/drop/dropsTheFirst
  |~~ Dropping two leaves the last three. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3 4 5] | drop 2 | eq [3 4 5])

:vec/drop/dropsTheWholePastTheLength
  |~~ A count past the length leaves the empty vector. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | drop 99 | eq [])

:vec/drop/dropsNothingByANegativeCount
  |~~ A negative count drops nothing. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | drop -1 | eq [1 2 3])

:vec/drop/keepsAQuoteAQuote
  |~~ A quote drops its first steps. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | drop 1 | eq ~(mul 2))

:vec/drop/count/NotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
  |~~ A fraction is no count. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | drop 1.5 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/drop/count/NotIntegerError ::number])

:vec/drop/count/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A count written as a string is refused. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | drop "1" !| type | eq ::vec/drop/count/NotNumberError)

:vec/at
  |~~ Answers the element at the index the call gives, `[10 20 30] | at
      1` answering `20`, a negative index counting from the end, `at -1`
      the last. Past either end it answers the default the call gives,
      null when it gives none, so `at` reads a place softly where the
      step `/1` is refused; an element that is null answers null and not
      the default. The index is a whole number, and the default is
      computed against the subject, `at 99 (count)`. A set and a map
      answer `at` by words of their own, a map reading a key softly,
      `{:a 1} | at :b 0` answering `0`, and null answers the default.
      ::link~(::vec/first | doc) ::link~(::map/at | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/at/answer
  |~~ The element at the index, any value, or the default past either end. ~~|
  ::any

:vec/at/index
  |~~ The index, a whole number, a negative one counting from the end. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/at/index/NotGivenError
  |~~ `at` reads the element at the index its call gives, `[10 20] | at 1`;
      a call with no index is refused.
  ~~|

:vec/at/index/NotNumberError
  |~~ The index is a number, `[10 20] | at 1`; text that holds one is
      read by `toNumber` first, `at ("1" | toNumber)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:vec/at/index/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The index is a whole number, `[10 20] | at 1`; a fraction is
      rounded first, `at (0.6 | round)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (floor))

:vec/at/default
  |~~ The value answered for a place the subject does not hold, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 1)

:vec/at/readsTheIndex
  |~~ Index one is the second element. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | at 1 | eq 20)

:vec/at/countsFromTheEnd
  |~~ A negative index counts from the end. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | at -1 | eq 30)

:vec/at/answersNullPastTheEnd
  |~~ Past the end without a default null answers. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | at 99 | eq null)

:vec/at/answersTheDefaultPastTheEnd
  |~~ Past the end the default answers. ~~|
  ::law~([] | at 0 7 | eq 7)

:vec/at/answersTheDefaultBeforeTheStart
  |~~ Before the start the default answers. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | at -99 0 | eq 0)

:vec/at/readsSoftlyWhereAStepIsRefused
  |~~ A place the step refuses `at` answers softly. ~~|
  ::law~([10] | [(at 1) (/1 !| type)] | eq [null ::proj/IndexOutOfBoundsError])

:vec/at/keepsANullElement
  |~~ An element that is null answers null, not the default. ~~|
  ::law~([null] | at 0 7 | eq null)

:vec/at/computesTheDefaultAgainstTheSubject
  |~~ The default is computed against the subject. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | at 99 (count) | eq 3)

:vec/at/index/NotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
  |~~ A fraction is no index. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | at 1.5 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/at/index/NotIntegerError ::number])

:vec/at/index/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ An index written as a string is refused. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | at "1" !| type | eq ::vec/at/index/NotNumberError)

:vec/at/index/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoIndex
  |~~ A call with no index is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | at !| type | eq ::vec/at/index/NotGivenError)

:vec/distinct
  |~~ Answers the set of the elements, the vector in the one order
      without duplicates, `[2 1 2 3 1] | distinct` answering `#[1 2 3]`.
      Two elements are one when `eq` says so, so two maps, vectors or
      sets of the same content collapse and a number is one whatever its
      digits; the order of the subject is lost, since a set stands in the
      one order, and `count` after it counts the distinct values. The
      tags of the subject stay over the set, `::Box[2 1 2] | distinct`
      answering `::Box#[1 2]`. A set answers `distinct` by a word of its
      own.
      ::link~(::set | doc) ::link~(::any/eq | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/distinct/answer
  |~~ The elements, each once, in the one order: a set. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/distinct/answersASet
  |~~ The distinct elements are a set. ~~|
  ::law~([2 1 2 3 1] | distinct | eq #[1 2 3])

:vec/distinct/mergesEqualMaps
  |~~ Two maps of the same entries are one. ~~|
  ::law~([{:id 1} {:id 2} {:id 1}] | distinct | eq #[{:id 1} {:id 2}])

:vec/distinct/mergesANumberWhateverItsDigits
  |~~ A number is one whatever its digits. ~~|
  ::law~([1 1.0] | distinct | count | eq 1)

:vec/distinct/ordersByTheOneOrder
  |~~ The set's vector is in the one order. ~~|
  ::law~([2 1 2 3 1] | distinct | payload | eq [1 2 3])

:vec/distinct/countsTheDistinctValues
  |~~ A count after it counts the distinct values. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 2] | distinct | count | eq 2)

:vec/distinct/keepsTheTagOfTheSubject
  |~~ The tags of the subject stay over the set. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box[2 1 2] | distinct | eq ::Box#[1 2])

:vec/reverse
  |~~ Answers the elements in the reverse order, `[1 2 3] | reverse`
      answering `[3 2 1]`. The answer keeps the kind of the subject, a
      vector under a tag and a quote, whose steps it reverses; a set and
      a map answer `reverse` by words of their own, a set reversing into
      a vector.
      ::link~(::vec/sort | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/reverse/answer
  |~~ The elements in reverse order. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/reverse/reversesTheOrder
  |~~ The elements come in reverse order. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | reverse | eq [3 2 1])

:vec/reverse/keepsTheTagOfTheSubject
  |~~ A vector under a tag reverses under it. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box[1 2 3] | reverse | eq ::Box[3 2 1])

:vec/reverse/reversesTheStepsOfAQuote
  |~~ A quote reverses its steps. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | reverse | eq ~(mul 2 | add 1))

:vec/flat
  |~~ Answers the elements with one level of nesting spliced in, `[[1 2]
      [3] [4 5]] | flat` answering `[1 2 3 4 5]`. An element that is a
      vector splices its elements whatever tag it stands under, so a set,
      a quote and a doc splice too, a quote its steps and a doc its
      segments; a vector deeper inside stays whole, and an element that
      is no vector stays as it is, `[1 [2]] | flat` answering `[1 2]`. The
      answer keeps the tags of the subject, `::Box[[1] [2]] | flat`
      answering `::Box[1 2]`. A set answers `flat` by a word of its own;
      `append` puts a vector in as one element, and `flat` after it
      splices it.
      ::link~(::vec/append | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/flat/answer
  |~~ The elements with one level of nesting spliced in. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/flat/splicesOneLevel
  |~~ The inner vectors splice into one. ~~|
  ::law~([[1 2] [3] [4 5]] | flat | eq [1 2 3 4 5])

:vec/flat/splicesAVectorUnderATag
  |~~ A set and a vector under a tag splice. ~~|
  ::law~([#[2 1] ::Box[3]] | flat | eq [1 2 3])

:vec/flat/splicesTheStepsOfAQuote
  |~~ A quote splices its steps. ~~|
  ::law~([~(add 1) [2]] | flat | first | type | eq ::call)

:vec/flat/keepsADeeperVector
  |~~ A vector two levels in stays whole. ~~|
  ::law~([[1 [2]] 3] | flat | eq [1 [2] 3])

:vec/flat/keepsAnElementThatIsNoVector
  |~~ An element that is no vector stays as it is. ~~|
  ::law~([1 [2]] | flat | eq [1 2])

:vec/flat/keepsTheTagOfTheSubject
  |~~ The tags of the subject stay over the answer. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box[[1] [2]] | flat | eq ::Box[1 2])

:vec/zip
  |~~ Answers the vectors the subject holds read side by side, one vector
      of the elements at each index, as long as the shortest:
      `[[1 2] [:a :b]] | zip` answers `[[1 :a] [2 :b]]`, and the empty
      vector answers `[]`. Each element is a vector, a set, a quote and a
      doc among them; a single vector is put beside the others in a
      vector first, `[(/names) (/ages)] | zip`. It takes no value.
      ::link~(::vec/flat | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/zip/answer
  |~~ The elements at each index, a vector each. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/zip/ElementNotVecError
  |~~ `zip` reads vectors side by side; `:index` names the first element
      that is no vector, a vector put in a vector first, `[(/a) (/b)] |
      zip`.
  ~~|

:vec/zip/pairsTheElementsAtEachIndex
  |~~ The elements at each index stand together. ~~|
  ::law~([[1 2] [:a :b]] | zip | eq [[1 :a] [2 :b]])

:vec/zip/stopsAtTheShortest
  |~~ The answer is as long as the shortest vector. ~~|
  ::law~([[1 2 3] [:a]] | zip | eq [[1 :a]])

:vec/zip/answersNothingOfNothing
  |~~ The empty vector answers the empty vector. ~~|
  ::law~([] | zip | eq [])

:vec/zip/readsFieldsSideBySide
  |~~ Two fields of a record read side by side pair their elements. ~~|
  ::law~({:names ["ann" "bob"] :ages [30 40]} | [(/names) (/ages)] | zip | eq [["ann" 30] ["bob" 40]])

:vec/zip/ElementNotVecError/refusesANumber
  |~~ An element that is no vector is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~([[1 2] 3] | zip !| [type /index /actualType] | eq [::vec/zip/ElementNotVecError 1 ::number])


:vec/reduce
  |~~ Answers the seed folded over the elements by the reducer, each
      element folded into the value so far, `[1 2 3 4 5] | reduce 0
      ~(add)` answering `15`; the empty vector answers the seed. The seed
      comes first and the reducer after it, so `reduce ~(add)` reads the
      quote as the seed and is refused for the reducer it lacks. The
      reducer is a quote. A quote of one word, `~(add)` or `~add`, or of
      its address, `~(number/add)`, is the word the fold calls with the
      value so far as its subject and the element as its first arg; any
      other code runs on the pair of the value so far and the element,
      and what it answers is the value so far after that element:
      `[{:x 1} {:x 2}] | reduce 0 ~(sumOf /0 (/1 | /x))` answers `3`. A
      failure the reducer answers answers the call. `sum`, `count`, `max`
      and the builders of structures all
      factor through it: adding from zero sums, multiplying from one
      gives the product, appending joins strings and uniting merges sets.
      A set and a map answer `reduce` by words of their own.
      ::link~(::vec/sum | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/reduce/answer
  |~~ The value folded after the last element, any value, the seed for an empty vector. ~~|
  ::any

:vec/reduce/seed
  |~~ The value the fold starts from, any value. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/reduce/reducer
  |~~ A quote: one word the fold calls with the element, or code it runs on the pair of the value so far and the element. ~~|
  ~(/1)

:vec/reduce/reducer/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The reducer is a quote, `reduce 0 ~(add)`, after the seed: written
      first, `reduce ~(add) 0`, it stands in the seed's place and hands
      the reducer's place the seed, and written without the tilde,
      `(add)`, it is computed once at the call; either is refused.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:vec/reduce/reducer/NotGivenError
  |~~ `reduce` takes the seed first and the reducer after it, `reduce 0
      ~(add)`; a call of the reducer alone reads it as the seed.
  ~~|

:vec/reduce/sums
  |~~ Adding from zero sums. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3 4 5] | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 15)

:vec/reduce/multiplies
  |~~ Multiplying from one gives the product. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3 4 5] | reduce 1 ~(mul) | eq 120)

:vec/reduce/joinsStrings
  |~~ Appending from the empty string joins. ~~|
  ::law~(["a" "b" "c"] | reduce "" ~(append) | eq "abc")

:vec/reduce/unitesSets
  |~~ Uniting from the empty set merges. ~~|
  ::law~([#[1] #[2 3]] | reduce #[] ~(union) | eq #[1 2 3])

:vec/reduce/takesTheQuoteOfAName
  |~~ The quote of a name alone is a reducer. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~add | eq 6)

:vec/reduce/takesTheAddressOfAWord
  |~~ A word named by its address is a reducer. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(number/add) | eq 6)

:vec/reduce/runsCodeOnThePair
  |~~ Code of more than one word runs on the pair of the value so far and the element. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(:acc /0 | :el /1 | acc | mul 10 | add el) | eq 123)

:vec/reduce/foldsAFieldOfEachElement
  |~~ Code reads a field of each element from the pair. ~~|
  ::law~([{:x 1} {:x 2}] | reduce 0 ~(sumOf /0 (/1 | /x)) | eq 3)

:vec/reduce/takesAName
  |~~ A name takes the value so far as its subject and the element as its first arg. ~~|
  ::law~(:number/max2 ~(if (gt x) ~(/) ~(x))
    | :number/max2/x |~~ The element. ~~| ~(/0)
    | :number/max2/x/NotNumberError ::holds~(is ::number)
    | [3 1 4 1 5] | reduce 0 ~max2
    | eq 5)

:vec/reduce/takesANameThatLeavesTheElement
  |~~ A name may leave the element unread. ~~|
  ::law~(:number/tally ~(add 1)
    | :number/tally/el |~~ The element, unread. ~~| ~(/0)
    | [10 20 30] | reduce 0 ~tally
    | eq 3)

:vec/reduce/answersTheSeedOfNothing
  |~~ The seed is the first value so far, answered as it is for an empty subject. ~~|
  ::law~([] | reduce 42 ~(add) | eq 42)

:vec/reduce/answersTheErrorOfTheReducer
  |~~ An error the reducer answers answers the call. ~~|
  ::law~([1 "x" 3] | reduce 0 ~(add) !| type | eq ::number/add/addend/NotNumberError)

:vec/reduce/reducer/NotQuoteError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number computed at the call is refused. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | reduce 0 1
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::vec/reduce/reducer/NotQuoteError ::number])

:vec/reduce/reducer/NotQuoteError/refusesTheReducerFirst
  |~~ A reducer written before the seed hands the reducer's place the seed. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | reduce ~(add) 0
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::vec/reduce/reducer/NotQuoteError ::number])

:vec/reduce/reducer/NotGivenError/readsALoneQuoteAsTheSeed
  |~~ A call of the reducer alone lacks the reducer. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | reduce ~(add) !| type | eq ::vec/reduce/reducer/NotGivenError)

:vec/reduce/takesANameOfTwoSteps
  |~~ Two steps under a name of their own fold as one word. ~~|
  ::law~(:number/step ~(mul 2 | add el)
    | :number/step/el ~(/0)
    | :number/step/el/NotNumberError ::holds~(is ::number)
    | [1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(step)
    | eq 11)

:vec/union
  |~~ Answers the union of the values the vector holds, from the first:
      sets unite in the one order, `[#[:a :b] #[:b :c]] | union`
      answering `#[:a :b :c]`, maps merge with the last value winning a
      key, `[{:a 1} {:a 2 :b 3}] | union` answering `{:a 2 :b 3}`, and a
      vector of one value answers it. The values are sets or maps: one
      that is neither is refused at its index, a set beside a map with the
      pair, and the vector holds one value at least. The call takes no
      value, and two values unite as `a | union b`.
      ::link~(::vec/minus | doc) ::link~(::vec/inter | doc)
      ::link~(::set/union | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/union/answer
  |~~ The union of the values, a set or a map. ~~|
  #[::set ::map]

:vec/union/SubjectEmptyError
  |~~ `union` over a vector unites its values from the first, `[a b c]
      | union`, so the vector holds one at least; two values unite as
      `a | union b`.
  ~~|

:vec/union/ElementNotSetOrMapError
  |~~ The values `union` unites are sets or maps; `:index` names the first
      that is neither, and a vector of vectors unites once each is a set,
      `map ~(distinct) | union`.
  ~~|

:vec/union/KindsMismatchedError
  |~~ The values `union` unites are all sets or all maps; a set beside a
      map is refused with the pair.
  ~~|

:vec/union/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `union` over a vector unites the values it holds and takes no value:
      two values unite as `a | union b`, `#[1] | union #[2]`.
  ~~|

:vec/union/unitesTheValues
  |~~ The sets the vector holds unite. ~~|
  ::law~([#[:a :b] #[:b :c]] | union | eq #[:a :b :c])

:vec/union/mergesMapsTheLastWinning
  |~~ Maps merge with the last value winning a key. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 1} {:a 2 :b 3}] | union | eq {:a 2 :b 3})

:vec/union/answersAValueAlone
  |~~ A vector of one value answers it. ~~|
  ::law~([#[:a]] | union | eq #[:a])

:vec/union/SubjectEmptyError/refusesTheEmptyVector
  |~~ The empty vector holds no value. ~~|
  ::law~([] | union !| type | eq ::vec/union/SubjectEmptyError)

:vec/union/ElementNotSetOrMapError/refusesANumberAtItsIndex
  |~~ A value that is neither a set nor a map is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~([#[:a] 2] | union
    !| [type /index /actualType]
    |  eq [::vec/union/ElementNotSetOrMapError 1 ::number])

:vec/union/ElementNotSetOrMapError/unitesVectorsMadeSets
  |~~ A vector of vectors unites once each is a set. ~~|
  ::law~([[1 2] [2 3]] | map ~(distinct) | union | eq #[1 2 3])

:vec/union/KindsMismatchedError/refusesASetAndAMap
  |~~ A set and a map do not unite. ~~|
  ::law~([#[:a] {:b 1}] | union
    !| [type /leftType /rightType]
    |  eq [::vec/union/KindsMismatchedError ::set ::map])

:vec/union/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAValueGiven
  |~~ A value the call gives is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~([#[1]] | union #[2] !| type | eq ::vec/union/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:vec/minus
  |~~ Answers the first value the vector holds less each one after it:
      a set loses the elements the next set holds, `[#[:a :b :c] #[:b]]
      | minus` answering `#[:a :c]`, and a map loses the keys of the
      next, which is a map, a set or a vector of keys, each a keyword or
      a string, `[{:a 1 :b 2} [:a]] | minus` answering `{:b 2}`. The
      first value is a set or a map, refused at its index when it is
      neither, a value after it the algebra has no rule for is refused
      with the pair, and the vector holds one value at least. The call
      takes no value, and of two values `a | minus b` takes `b` from `a`.
      ::link~(::vec/union | doc) ::link~(::vec/inter | doc)
      ::link~(::set/minus | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/minus/answer
  |~~ The first value less the ones after it, a set or a map. ~~|
  #[::set ::map]

:vec/minus/SubjectEmptyError
  |~~ `minus` over a vector takes from its first value the ones after it,
      `[a b c] | minus`, so the vector holds one at least; of two values
      `a | minus b` takes `b` from `a`.
  ~~|

:vec/minus/ElementNotSetOrMapError
  |~~ The value `minus` takes from is a set or a map; `:index` names the
      first value of the vector, which is neither.
  ~~|

:vec/minus/KindsMismatchedError
  |~~ After a set `minus` takes a set, and after a map a map, a set or a
      vector of keys; another value is refused with the pair.
  ~~|

:vec/minus/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError
  |~~ A key `minus` takes out of a map is a keyword or a string; `:index`
      names the first element of the vector of keys that is neither.
  ~~|

:vec/minus/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `minus` over a vector takes from its first value the ones after it
      and takes no value: of two values `a | minus b` takes `b` from `a`,
      `#[1 2] | minus #[2]`.
  ~~|

:vec/minus/subtractsTheRest
  |~~ The first set loses what the next holds. ~~|
  ::law~([#[:a :b :c] #[:b]] | minus | eq #[:a :c])

:vec/minus/takesKeysOutOfAMap
  |~~ A map loses the keys of a vector after it, a string among them. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 1 :b 2} ["a"]] | minus | eq {:b 2})

:vec/minus/takesTheKeysOfAMap
  |~~ A map loses the keys of a map after it. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 1 :b 2} {:a 3}] | minus | eq {:b 2})

:vec/minus/SubjectEmptyError/refusesTheEmptyVector
  |~~ The empty vector holds no value. ~~|
  ::law~([] | minus !| type | eq ::vec/minus/SubjectEmptyError)

:vec/minus/ElementNotSetOrMapError/refusesANumberFirst
  |~~ A first value that is neither a set nor a map is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~([1 #[1]] | minus !| [type /index] | eq [::vec/minus/ElementNotSetOrMapError 0])

:vec/minus/KindsMismatchedError/refusesAMapAfterASet
  |~~ A map after a set is refused with the pair. ~~|
  ::law~([#[:a] {:b 1}] | minus
    !| [type /leftType /rightType]
    |  eq [::vec/minus/KindsMismatchedError ::set ::map])

:vec/minus/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number among the keys is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 1} [:a 1]] | minus
    !| [type /actualType /index]
    |  eq [::vec/minus/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError ::number 1])

:vec/minus/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAValueGiven
  |~~ A value the call gives is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~([#[1]] | minus #[2] !| type | eq ::vec/minus/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:vec/inter
  |~~ Answers what the values the vector holds share: a set keeps the
      elements every set after it holds, `[#[:a :b :c] #[:b :d]] | inter`
      answering `#[:b]`, and a map keeps the keys of the next, which is a
      map, a set or a vector of keys, with its own values, `[{:a 1 :b 2}
      {:a 3}] | inter` answering `{:a 1}`. The first value is a set or a
      map, refused at its index when it is neither, a value after it the
      algebra has no rule for is refused with the pair, and the vector
      holds one value at least. The call takes no value, and of two
      values `a | inter b` keeps what `a` shares with `b`.
      ::link~(::vec/union | doc) ::link~(::vec/minus | doc)
      ::link~(::set/inter | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/inter/answer
  |~~ What every value holds, a set or a map. ~~|
  #[::set ::map]

:vec/inter/SubjectEmptyError
  |~~ `inter` over a vector keeps what its values share from the first,
      `[a b c] | inter`, so the vector holds one at least; of two values
      `a | inter b` keeps what `a` shares with `b`.
  ~~|

:vec/inter/ElementNotSetOrMapError
  |~~ The value `inter` keeps from is a set or a map; `:index` names the
      first value of the vector, which is neither.
  ~~|

:vec/inter/KindsMismatchedError
  |~~ After a set `inter` takes a set, and after a map a map, a set or a
      vector of keys; another value is refused with the pair.
  ~~|

:vec/inter/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError
  |~~ A key `inter` keeps of a map is a keyword or a string; `:index`
      names the first element of the vector of keys that is neither.
  ~~|

:vec/inter/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `inter` over a vector keeps what its values share and takes no
      value: of two values `a | inter b` keeps what `a` shares with `b`,
      `#[1 2] | inter #[2]`.
  ~~|

:vec/inter/keepsTheCommonElements
  |~~ The elements both sets hold stay. ~~|
  ::law~([#[:a :b :c] #[:b :d]] | inter | eq #[:b])

:vec/inter/keepsTheKeysOfAMapWithItsValues
  |~~ A map keeps the keys of a map after it, with its own values. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 1 :b 2} {:a 3}] | inter | eq {:a 1})

:vec/inter/keepsTheKeysOfASet
  |~~ A map keeps the keys a set after it holds. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 1 :b 2} #[:a]] | inter | eq {:a 1})

:vec/inter/SubjectEmptyError/refusesTheEmptyVector
  |~~ The empty vector holds no value. ~~|
  ::law~([] | inter !| type | eq ::vec/inter/SubjectEmptyError)

:vec/inter/ElementNotSetOrMapError/refusesANumberFirst
  |~~ A first value that is neither a set nor a map is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~([1 #[1]] | inter !| [type /index] | eq [::vec/inter/ElementNotSetOrMapError 0])

:vec/inter/KindsMismatchedError/refusesAMapAfterASet
  |~~ A map after a set is refused with the pair. ~~|
  ::law~([#[:a] {:b 1}] | inter
    !| [type /leftType /rightType]
    |  eq [::vec/inter/KindsMismatchedError ::set ::map])

:vec/inter/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError/refusesAVector
  |~~ A vector among the keys is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~([{:a 1} [[:a]]] | inter
    !| [type /actualType /index]
    |  eq [::vec/inter/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError ::vec 0])

:vec/inter/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAValueGiven
  |~~ A value the call gives is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~([#[1]] | inter #[2] !| type | eq ::vec/inter/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:vec/join
  |~~ Answers the strings and keywords the vector holds joined into one, the separator
      the call gives between every two, `["a" "b" "c"] | join ","`
      answering `"a,b,c"`; the empty vector joins to the empty string,
      and `split` takes the string apart again. Every element is a
      string or a keyword, written as its name, so the pairs of a map of
      strings join as they stand, `{:host "x"} | entries | map ~(join
      "=")` answering `["host=x"]`; another value is refused at its index,
      and values are made text first, `map ~print | join "+"`. A set joins
      its elements in the one order; a map, a string or a number is
      refused under the word, and a map's values are read first, `vals |
      join ","`.
      ::link~(::string/split | doc) ::link~(::any/print | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/join/answer
  |~~ The elements joined into one string by the separator. ~~|
  ::string

:vec/join/separator
  |~~ The text written between every two elements, `join ", "`. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/join/separator/NotStringOrKeywordError
  |~~ The separator is a string, `["a" "b"] | join ","`, or a keyword
      written as its name; a number writes as text by `toString` first,
      `join (1 | toString)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string ::keyword)

:vec/join/SubjectNotVecError
  |~~ `join` writes a vector of strings as one: a map's values are read
      first, `vals | join ","`, and a string is taken apart by `split`.
  ~~|

:vec/join/ElementNotStringOrKeywordError
  |~~ Every element `join` writes is a string or a keyword, written as
      its name; `:index` names the first that is neither, and `map
      ~print` makes each value its text first.
  ~~|

:vec/join/joinsWithTheSeparator
  |~~ The separator stands between the strings. ~~|
  ::law~(["a" "b" "c"] | join "," | eq "a,b,c")

:vec/join/joinsNothingToTheEmptyString
  |~~ The empty vector joins to the empty string. ~~|
  ::law~([] | join "," | eq "")

:vec/join/isUndoneBySplit
  |~~ `split` takes the joined string apart again. ~~|
  ::law~(["a" "b"] | join "," | split "," | eq ["a" "b"])

:vec/join/writesAKeywordAsItsName
  |~~ The pairs of the entries of a map of strings join, each key written as its name. ~~|
  ::law~({:host "x" :port "80"} | entries | map ~(join "=") | eq ["host=x" "port=80"])

:vec/join/writesAKeywordSeparatorAsItsName
  |~~ A keyword separator is written as its name. ~~|
  ::law~(["a" "b"] | join :x | eq "axb")

:vec/join/joinsValuesMadeText
  |~~ Values made text first join. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | map ~print | join "+" | eq "1+2")

:vec/join/joinsASetInTheOneOrder
  |~~ A set joins its elements in the one order. ~~|
  ::law~(#["b" "a"] | join "," | eq "a,b")

:vec/join/joinsTheValuesOfAMap
  |~~ The values of a map are read first. ~~|
  ::law~({:a "x" :b "y"} | vals | join "," | eq "x,y")

:vec/join/separator/NotStringOrKeywordError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number separator is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(["a" "b"] | join 42
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::vec/join/separator/NotStringOrKeywordError ::number])

:vec/join/SubjectNotVecError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no vector of strings. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | join "," !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/join/SubjectNotVecError ::number])

:vec/join/SubjectNotVecError/refusesAMap
  |~~ A map is no vector of strings. ~~|
  ::law~({:a "x"} | join "," !| [type /actualType] | eq [::vec/join/SubjectNotVecError ::map])

:vec/join/ElementNotStringOrKeywordError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number among the strings is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~(["a" 42 "c"] | join ","
    !| [type /actualType /index]
    |  eq [::vec/join/ElementNotStringOrKeywordError ::number 1])

:vec/prepend
  |~~ Answers the vector with the element the call gives at its head,
      any value, `[2 3] | prepend 1` answering `[1 2 3]`; a vector given
      is one element, `[1] | prepend [0]` answering `[[0] 1]`. The answer
      keeps the kind of the subject: a vector under a tag stays under it,
      a quote takes the element as its first step, and a set takes it
      through its constructor, so it stays a set in the one order. A
      string answers `prepend` by a word of its own; `append` puts the
      element at the end.
      ::link~(::vec/append | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/prepend/answer
  |~~ The vector with the element as its first. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/prepend/element
  |~~ The element put at the head, any value. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/prepend/putsTheElementFirst
  |~~ The element goes first. ~~|
  ::law~([2 3] | prepend 1 | eq [1 2 3])

:vec/prepend/putsAVectorInAsOneElement
  |~~ A vector given is one element. ~~|
  ::law~([1] | prepend [0] | eq [[0] 1])

:vec/prepend/keepsTheTagOfTheSubject
  |~~ A vector under a tag takes the element under it. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box[2] | prepend 1 | eq ::Box[1 2])

:vec/prepend/keepsASetASet
  |~~ A set takes the element as a set does. ~~|
  ::law~(#[2 3] | prepend 3 | eq #[2 3])

:vec/append
  |~~ Answers the vector with the element the call gives at its end, any
      value, `[1 2] | append 3` answering `[1 2 3]`; a vector given is one
      element, so `[1] | append [2 3]` answers `[1 [2 3]]`, and `flat`
      after it splices the vector in. The answer keeps the kind of the
      subject: a vector under a tag stays under it, a quote takes the
      element as its last step, a set takes it through its constructor,
      so it stays a set, and a doc takes a segment, a string or a quote,
      and refuses another value. A string answers `append` by a word of
      its own; `prepend` puts the element at the head.
      ::link~(::vec/prepend | doc) ::link~(::vec/flat | doc)
      ::link~(::doc/ElementNotSegmentError | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/append/answer
  |~~ The vector with the element as its last. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/append/element
  |~~ The element put at the end, any value. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:vec/append/putsTheElementLast
  |~~ The element goes last. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | append 3 | eq [1 2 3])

:vec/append/putsAVectorInAsOneElement
  |~~ A vector given is one element, which `flat` splices. ~~|
  ::law~([1] | append [2 3] | [/ (flat)] | eq [[1 [2 3]] [1 2 3]])

:vec/append/keepsTheTagOfTheSubject
  |~~ A vector under a tag takes the element under it. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box[1 2] | append 3 | eq ::Box[1 2 3])

:vec/append/keepsASetASet
  |~~ A set takes the element as a set does. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | append 1 | eq #[1 2])

:vec/toSet
  |~~ Answers the set of the vector's elements, `[2 1 2] | toSet`
      answering `#[1 2]`: the one order and no duplicates, as `distinct`
      answers. `toVec` turns the set back into a vector. A map's keys are
      a set already, `keys`.
      ::link~(::vec/distinct | doc) ::link~(::set/toVec | doc)
      ::link~(::map/keys | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/toSet/answer
  |~~ The set of the elements. ~~|
  ::set

:vec/toSet/leadsAMapToItsKeys
  |~~ A map is led to its keys. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | toSet !| /nearest | eq [:keys])

:vec/toSet/answersTheSetDistinctAnswers
  |~~ `toSet` answers what `distinct` answers. ~~|
  ::law~([2 1 2] | [toSet distinct] | eq [#[1 2] #[1 2]])

:vec/toMap
  |~~ Answers the map the vector's pairs `[key value]` write, the reverse
      of `entries`, `[[:a 1] [:b 2]] | toMap` answering `{:a 1 :b 2}`, a
      set of pairs alike. Each key is named as `toKeyword` names it,
      `[[1 "x"]] | toMap` answering `{:"1" "x"}`, and of two pairs with one
      key the last holds. An element that is no pair and a key no keyword
      names are refused at their index.
      ::link~(::map/entries | doc) ::link~(::string/toKeyword | doc)
      ::link~(::map | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/toMap/answer
  |~~ The map of the pairs. ~~|
  ::map

:vec/toMap/ElementNotPairError
  |~~ Each element of the vector `toMap` reads is a pair `[key value]`;
      `:index` names the first that is not.
  ~~|

:vec/toMap/KeyNotKeywordError
  |~~ The key of a pair is a value `toKeyword` names, a keyword, a string,
      a number, a boolean, null or a tag name; `:index` names the pair
      whose key is another value.
  ~~|

:vec/toMap/readsBack
  |~~ A map turned into its pairs turns back into itself. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | entries | toMap | eq {:a 1 :b 2})

:vec/toMap/readsASetOfPairs
  |~~ A set of pairs builds a map as a vector of them does. ~~|
  ::law~(#[[:a 1]] | toMap | eq {:a 1})

:vec/toMap/namesEachKeyAsToKeywordNamesIt
  |~~ A number and a boolean name the keywords of their texts. ~~|
  ::law~([[1 "x"] [true 2]] | toMap | eq {:"1" "x" :true 2})

:vec/toMap/keepsTheLastValueOfAKeyWrittenTwice
  |~~ A key written twice among the pairs holds its last value. ~~|
  ::law~([[:a 1] [:a 2]] | toMap | eq {:a 2})

:vec/toMap/ElementNotPairError/refusesAPairOfOne
  |~~ An element of one place is no pair, and is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~([[:a 1] [:b]] | toMap !| [type /index] | eq [::vec/toMap/ElementNotPairError 1])

:vec/toMap/ElementNotPairError/refusesAPairOfThree
  |~~ An element of three places is no pair either. ~~|
  ::law~([[:a 1 2]] | toMap !| [type /index] | eq [::vec/toMap/ElementNotPairError 0])

:vec/toMap/KeyNotKeywordError/refusesAVectorKey
  |~~ A vector names no key, and is refused at its pair. ~~|
  ::law~([[[1] 2]] | toMap !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::vec/toMap/KeyNotKeywordError ::vec 0])

:vec/toVec
  |~~ Answers the vector itself, so `map ~toVec` turns vectors and sets
      into vectors.
      ::link~(::set/toVec | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/toVec/answer
  |~~ The vector itself. ~~|
  ::vec

:vec/toVec/answersItself
  |~~ A vector answers itself. ~~|
  ::law~([2 1] | toVec | eq [2 1])
